
SART is a temperature reduction ratio in simmulated annealing.
It MUST range from 0.0 and 1.0. Any value outside this range
defaults to 0.5.
An SART value of 0.0 means that the temperature will drop to
0 K in one step and therefore the energy minimization will take
only a downhill path. On the other hand, a value of 1.0 will
not reduce the temperature at all and the minimization will
not converge.
Normally you would use a default value 0.5. But if the potential
energy surface is rather flat or the initial geometry is too
far from a minimum, you would take a larger value so that
more surfaces can be scanned before the energy reaches a well.
Likewise, if the initial guess lies near the bottom, you can
take a smaller SART value to speed up the minimization.

